\mode<presentation>{%
  \begin{frame}
    \begin{block}{218 items became 198}
      \centering
      \footnotesize
      \banktable
    \end{block}
  \end{frame}
}

Of the 198, 94 are single choice, 47 multiple answers, 20 fill in the
blank, 19 ordering and 18 matching.  All 198 have a neutral, a right and a
wrong feedback text, and the options have 588 texts of their own.

\leavevmode\ltnote{%
  The defects are grouped by the check that found them, not by bank or
  by type, so that the four checks of \cref{sec:items} return as the
  structure of the results: the same dimension, now with the real
  values.
}%
The defects in the live banks, each confirmed by running code, fall into
the classes of \cref{sec:items} and a few more.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{Found by running}
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Keys that crash: the caller's variables used
        (\texttt{NameError}).
      \item A \enquote{not found} message never printed: a flag set
        outside its \texttt{if}.
      \item Wrong keys: a loop the key said gives 28 gives 21; two
        items with no correct option.
      \item Options that can never work: an \texttt{IndentationError},
        arguments \texttt{print} does not have.
      \item \texttt{except} for errors never raised at run time
        (\texttt{SyntaxError}).
      \item A harmful fix as key: catching \texttt{TypeError} to hide a
        missing \texttt{int()}.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

The last of these is worth a moment.  The key did make the program
survive bad input --- by catching the exception that the missing
conversion caused, so that the program then rejected even valid
input.  An
item that rewards that fix teaches it.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{Found by trying orders, measuring, reading the bank}
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Ordering items with two valid orders, or none (a stray
        apostrophe, a double space).
      \item The key the longest option in nine of 11 and eight of 10 items
        of two banks; the same answer pattern in six of seven items of another.
      \item Exact duplicates, six in one bank alone; items in the wrong
        bank.
      \item A note to a colleague, visible to students; real staff names
        and e-mail addresses in one item.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

The reviews of the rewritten banks found no blocker.  The six majors were
cues (option length, and a near-identical pair of options that always
contained the key), an ambiguity that a student running the code would
hit, a made-up personal identity number (\foreignlanguage{swedish}{%
personnummer}), and a fill-in item that tested the wrong objective.  None
was a wrong key.


\section[Round 2]{A second round: an independent exam, copy and paste,
  and time}
\label{sec:second-round}

\leavevmode\ltnote{%
  Relevant learning objectives: \cref{RapportLOFlaws,RapportLOWorkflow}.

  The second round is told in the order it happened: a comparison
  exposed a property of the banks that none of the four checks of
  \cref{sec:items} looks for, and the colleagues meet it as a try-first
  question (\cref{ex:paste}) before the numbers.  The adversarial review
  is a second try-first (\cref{ex:beat}) on an invented item in the
  reworked format, so that the ways around it are found, not listed.

  \textbf{Variation pattern}: contrast in one dimension, what a Python
  terminal can answer: an item it answers (\cref{ex:paste}), an item it
  does not answer by running the example (\cref{ex:format-h}), an item
  whose answer it shows but not the reason (\cref{ex:format-n}); and
  once on real, public items (\cref{ex:pair}), a sibling of an item
  before the rework against the item after it, so that the invented
  contrast is seen to hold for the banks.
  \textbf{What stays invariant}: short programs on taught topics.
}%
The banks had passed four kinds of check when a second look, a day
later, found something none of them looks for.  This section is
that second round: what an exam written without the banks did
differently, the property that comparison exposed, the rework that
followed, the ways around it and a recount after it, and how long the
exam now takes to read.
Like the lessons, it is this case's experience.

\mode<article>{\subsection{An exam written without the banks}%
\label{sec:comparison}}

One agent had written an exam from the course material alone, without
seeing the banks (\cref{sec:rest}); it is printed in full in
\cref{sec:ep2}, next to the course's earlier practice exam, Exempelprov~1,
which was built from the old shared banks with one item per position
(\cref{sec:ep1}).  A comparison agent then coded all 219
items, 198 from the banks and 21 from the independent exam, on a scheme
written before any coding: cognitive level, the role of the exam's Python
terminal, the context of the item, the rationale of the distractors, and
length.  A second coder, of the same model family, coded 51 items again;
the agreement (Cohen's $\kappa$) was 0.97 for level, 0.83 for the
terminal's role, 0.74 for context and 0.90 for the distractors.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{The banks against an exam written without them}
    Coded on a scheme written first; a second coder on 51 items
    ($\kappa$ 0.74--0.97).
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Pasting the code into the terminal gives the key: 88 of 198
        bank items (44\,\%), six of 21 independent ones.
      \item Pure recall: 23\,\% against none; filler distractors: 13 items
        against none.
      \item Constructs from two weeks or more: 27\,\% against 86\,\%.
      \item Write a program and run it: none against two.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

The independent exam was not better at everything: it took about 1.7
times as much reading per sitting.  But it differed in kind.  Two of its
items asked the student to write and run a program, one following a
\texttt{while} sequence for 113 steps, one counting 500 numbers with a
dictionary (questions 5 and 16, \cref{ep2:q5,ep2:q16}), and none of the
banks did.  Its items combined constructs
from two or more weeks of the course far more often.  And in 88 of the
198 bank items, pasting the shown code into the exam's terminal gave the
key; in three learning objectives a student could reach two of three in
every possible draw that way.  Much of this was inherited: the rewrite
had kept each bank's formats, and the banks are older than the exam's
Python terminal, which was added in October 2026.

\mode<article>{\subsection{Copy, paste, run}\label{sec:paste}}

The terminal is there so that students can check their reasoning, as they
would when programming.  Before reading what the comparison counted, try
the item yourself.

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{exercise}[Item 5]\label{ex:paste}
    The item shows a program and asks what it prints:
\begin{minted}{python}
total = 0
for n in [3, 1, 4, 1, 5]:
    if n > 2:
        total += n
print(total)
\end{minted}
    The options are \texttt{14}, \texttt{12}, \texttt{3} and \texttt{5}.
    A Python terminal is open beside the quiz.  What does the item
    measure?
  \end{exercise}
\end{frame}

The item is correct, and on paper it tests tracing a loop with a
condition.  With a terminal it tests something else.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{solution}[Item 5: copy, paste, run]
    Pasting the program into the terminal prints \texttt{12}.  The item
    measures whether the student can copy and paste.  The author:
    \enquote{Copy-pasting code to answer the question is not really
    testing any skill beyond being able to copy and paste and run the
    code.  Not what we want to measure.}
  \end{solution}
\end{frame}

The one study we found that watched students answer tracing items with an
interpreter at hand saw exactly this: on computer-based exams
\textcquote[115]{HassanZilles2021}{students can achieve significantly
higher scores on tracing problems than they do on similar paper-based
questions, through brute-force execution of the provided code}, and in
its small think-aloud sample there was \enquote{effectively no
correlation} between the two formats.%
\footnote{A think-aloud study with five tracing sessions; its authors are
\enquote{cautiously optimistic} about reverse tracing, items that give the
output and ask for an input.  The scientific basis is in
\cref{app:komplement}, which asks what an exam with a Python terminal can
still measure, and what makes a pool of items fair.}  Running code shows
the output, not the reason for it: students with output alone corrected
errors in the data flow but not in the control
flow.\autocite{HassanZilles2022}  The author decided to rework the 88
items.

\mode<article>{\subsection{Two formats a terminal does not answer}}

The 88 items in 11 banks were reworked into two formats.  In the first,
H, the stem says what a function should do and gives an example, and the
question is which version is right for every input: the example passes
at least two of the options, and an unnamed, natural case separates them.
In the second, N, the output is shown and the question is why: every
false explanation agrees with the output and with a quick run.  Filler
distractors and feedback that named the key were fixed in the same round,
and items that ask the student to write and run a program were postponed
to the next sitting.

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{example}[Format H, invented]\label{ex:format-h}
    \mode<presentation>{\setminted{fontsize=\scriptsize}}%
    \enquote{\texttt{largest} should return the largest number of a
    non-empty list; \texttt{largest([3, 7, 2])} returns \texttt{7}.
    Which version is right for every list?}
\begin{minted}{python}
def largest(numbers):
    best = ...    # (1)
    for n in ...: # (2)
        if n > best:
            best = n
    return best
\end{minted}
    The options fill the gaps:
    (A)~\texttt{0}, \texttt{numbers};
    (B)~\texttt{numbers[0]}, \texttt{numbers};
    (C)~\texttt{numbers[0]}, \texttt{numbers[:-1]};
    (D)~\texttt{numbers[-1]}, \texttt{numbers[1:]}.
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

The function is shown once, with gaps, and the options carry only the
lines that differ; we come back to why in \cref{sec:time}.  Running the
four versions on the stem's example and on a few ordinary lists shows how
the item works: every version passes the example, and each wrong one
fails one natural case --- a list of negative numbers, a largest number
last or first.

\begin{frame}<article>[fragile]
  \begin{example}[The four versions on four lists]
    A small program builds each version and runs it:
<<[[versions.py]]>>=
"""Run four versions of largest on the stem's example and other lists"""

TEMPLATE = """
def largest(numbers):
    best = {start}
    for n in {loop}:
        if n > best:
            best = n
    return best
"""
VERSIONS = {
    "A": ("0", "numbers"),
    "B": ("numbers[0]", "numbers"),
    "C": ("numbers[0]", "numbers[:-1]"),
    "D": ("numbers[-1]", "numbers[1:]"),
}
CASES = [[3, 7, 2], [-3, -1], [1, 2, 9], [9, 1, 2]]


def version(start, loop):
    """Return the function largest with the gaps filled in"""
    namespace = {}
    exec(TEMPLATE.format(start=start, loop=loop), namespace)
    return namespace["largest"]


def main():
    """Print what each version returns for each list"""
    print(f"{'list':<12}" + "".join(f"{name:>4}" for name in VERSIONS))
    for case in CASES:
        row = f"{str(case):<12}"
        for start, loop in VERSIONS.values():
            row += f"{version(start, loop)(case):>4}"
        print(row)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
@
    Its run:
    \runpython[showcommand]{examples/versions.py}
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{example}[Format N, invented]\label{ex:format-n}
    \mode<presentation>{\setminted{fontsize=\scriptsize}}%
    The program and its output are shown:
\begin{minted}{python}
counts = {}
for word in ["cat", "dog", "cat"]:
    counts[word] = counts.get(word, 0) + 1
print(counts)  # {'cat': 2, 'dog': 1}
\end{minted}
    \enquote{Why does \texttt{cat} get 2?}
    (A)~\texttt{get} gives 0 for a word not yet in \texttt{counts}, and
    each occurrence adds one.
    (B)~\texttt{get} first adds the word with the value 0, and the
    assignment then adds one.
    (C)~A loop over a list counts repeated elements, and \texttt{get}
    reads that count.
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

In format N, a run confirms the output but cannot tell the explanations
apart; the student has to know what [[get]] does.  Exempelprov 3, a draw
from the rewritten banks, has real items in both formats: question 4 is of
format H (\cref{ep3:q4}) and question 15 of format N
(\cref{ep3:q15}).  Question 4 was reworked from an item that asked how
many times a loop prints a word.  That item is not public, but
Exempelprov~1, the earlier practice exam, has a sibling of it: the same
template with other numbers, not the very item that was reworked
(\cref{ep1:q6}).  \Cref{ex:pair} shows the two side by side.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{example}[A real pair: before and after the rework]\label{ex:pair}
    \mode<presentation>{\setminted{fontsize=\tiny}\scriptsize}%
    On the left a sibling of the item before the rework, on the right the
    item after it.
    \def\pairbefore{%
      \textbf{Exempelprov 1, question 6}\only<article>{
      (\cref{ep1:q6})}: how many times is \texttt{hej} printed, with
      \texttt{antal = 25} before the loop?
      \inputminted{python}{examples/ep1-q6.txt}
      Options \texttt{25}, \texttt{24}, \texttt{15}, \texttt{14}; key
      \enquote{\foreignlanguage{swedish}{Inga gånger}} (none).}%
    \def\pairafter{%
      \textbf{Exempelprov 3, question 4}\only<article>{
      (\cref{ep3:q4})}: which condition makes \texttt{halvings} right for
      every whole number of 1 or more?  \texttt{halvings(15)} should give
      \texttt{3}.
      \inputminted[breaklines]{python}{examples/ep3-q4.txt}
      Options \texttt{while} with (A)~\texttt{number \% 2 == 0 and number
      > 2}; (B, key)~\texttt{number > 1}; (C)~\texttt{number > 2};
      (D)~\texttt{number // 2 > 1}.}%
    \mode<presentation>{\textbytext{\pairbefore}{\pairafter}}%
    \mode<article>{\textbytext*{\pairbefore}{\pairafter}}%
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

Pasting question 6 after [[antal = 25]] prints nothing: the run itself
shows the key, no lines.  Question 4 does not give way to pasting.  On
the stem's own example, 15, two of the four conditions give 3, and they
part only on a number that passes 2 on its way to 1, such as 16 or 2,
which the stem does not name.  The question is shown as the author's
review of this report left it, after the draw: the example became an odd
number, so that no student takes the halvings to work for even numbers
only, and option C was changed so that two options still pass the
example.  With the old option A beside it, the key would have been the
option closest to all the others, the centre cue of
\cref{sec:hypotheses}; the bank's own check rejected that, and A got a
second bug.

\begin{frame}<article>[fragile]
  \begin{example}[The pair run]
    A small program cuts the code of both questions from
    \cref{app:exempelprov}, runs question 6 and fills the gap of
    question 4 with each condition:
<<[[pair.py]]>>=
"""Run Exempelprov 1 question 6 and Exempelprov 3 question 4"""

import contextlib
import io
from pathlib import Path

HERE = Path(__file__).parent
CONDITIONS = {
    "A": "number % 2 == 0 and number > 2",
    "B": "number > 1",
    "C": "number > 2",
    "D": "number // 2 > 1",
}


def lines_printed():
    """Return how many lines question 6 prints after antal = 25"""
    code = "antal = 25\n" + (HERE / "ep1-q6.txt").read_text()
    output = io.StringIO()
    with contextlib.redirect_stdout(output):
        exec(code, {})
    return len(output.getvalue().splitlines())


def halvings(condition):
    """Return question 4's function with the gap filled by condition"""
    code = (HERE / "ep3-q4.txt").read_text()
    namespace = {}
    exec(code.replace("____", f"while {condition}:"), namespace)
    return namespace["halvings"]


def main():
    """Print the run of question 6 and the four versions of question 4"""
    print(f"Question 6 prints {lines_printed()} lines.")
    print("Question 4, halvings(n):")
    print(f"{'n':>4}" + "".join(f"{name:>4}" for name in CONDITIONS))
    for n in [15, 16, 2]:
        row = f"{n:>4}"
        for condition in CONDITIONS.values():
            row += f"{halvings(condition)(n):>4}"
        print(row)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
@
    Its run:
    \runpython[showcommand]{examples/pair.py}
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

Formats of this kind
have some support: items that give the output and ask for the input held
up better against an interpreter,\autocite{HassanZilles2021} and items
asking for the output of inputs that tracing alone cannot reach agreed
with explaining code in plain English and were more
reliable.\autocite{AlBluwi2025}  Whether H and N items measure what the
course wants is not known; the item statistics after the sitting will
give a first answer.

\mode<article>{\subsection{Adversarial review}}

The reworked items went through three rounds of review by agents told to
beat them: to find the key without the understanding the item asks for.
Try it on the invented item.

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{exercise}[Beat the format-H item]\label{ex:beat}
    Take the format-H item about \texttt{largest} again.  You do not know
    what goes wrong with negative numbers, but you have the terminal.
    How could you find the key anyway?
  \end{exercise}
\end{frame}

In this invented item one way needs no terminal at all: the key is the
majority of the options, gap by gap.  [[numbers[0]]] stands in two
options of four and [[numbers]] in two, and only B has both.  The three
rounds found that and six other ways around the first rework.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{solution}[Ways around the first rework]
    The three rounds found:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item A line-wise majority vote over the options rebuilds the key.
      \item A taught debug print, \texttt{print(f"\{line=\}")}, reveals
        the key.
      \item A near-twin of the key is killed by the stem's own example.
      \item A false statement contradicts a printed value.
      \item A wrong explanation suggests the experiment that refutes it.
      \item An option loops forever and hangs the exam's terminal.
      \item One new item has two defensible answers.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{solution}
\end{frame}

Each was fixed and the review repeated.  The last one is a reminder that
a rework makes new items, and new items have new defects.

\mode<article>{\subsection{Fixing cues creates cues}}

The first rework made the key \enquote{not the longest} in every item.
Applied item by item, that rule produced the opposite cue: in some banks
the key was never the longest nor the shortest option, so a student who
dropped both extremes was left with a guess between two.  Items cut to
three options had the key as the middle length.  What worked was a check
per bank, against chance, not a rule per item; the orchestrator's own
brief had stated the rule per item.  The same brief offered
write-and-run items although the author had said \enquote{not now}; one
rewriter wrote two, and they were turned into drafts for the next
sitting.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{Fixing cues creates cues}
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Rule per item: \enquote{the key is not the longest}.
      \item Result per bank: the key never the longest nor the shortest;
        drop both, and a guess between two is left.
      \item Three options: the key the middle length.
      \item The fix: a check per bank against chance, as the guessing
        rules do.
    \end{itemize}
    The orchestrator's own brief stated the rule per item, and offered
    write-and-run items the author had postponed.
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

\mode<article>{\subsection{A recount after the rework}\label{sec:recount}}

Two days after the comparison, agents coded all 198 items again on the
terminal's role alone, with the comparison's definition: an item gives
the key by pasting when running the shown code, or each option, on what
the item itself states and comparing the result with the stem leaves only
the key.  Every item with code was run.  Four items still gave the key
that way.  One was a reworked item of format H whose stem stated the
result for every input the function takes, 12 in all, so trying them all
left only the key.  The other three were not reworked: they asked which
calls of a function are fine, and every wrong call ended in an error, so
keeping the calls that ran gave the key.  These three had not changed
since the comparison, which coded them as items where the terminal only
helps, so its count of 88 was probably too low: under the recount's
coding it is at least 91.

The four were reworked after the recount, the same day: the one of
format H in three rounds, becoming a new item on the same objective, and
the three calls items in four, each round with adversarial review.  The
last round added a check to the bank build that tries every way of
finding the key by running alone that the reviews had found.  Coded
again, none of them gives the key by pasting: on each item's own
examples every option gives the stated result.  So no item of
the 198 now gives the key by pasting.%
\footnote{Near misses, coded as help: in the same bank as the three
calls items, five more items where the run removes the calls that crash
and the stem's own definition of a fine call removes the rest; and four
items of format H where the inputs that separate the options are the
limits the stem states (a weight, an age, a temperature).  The comparison
coded the independent exam's item with boundary ages the same way.}
Of the 88 reworked items, 87 now leave the terminal a helping role:
running rules out some options, but which inputs to try, or which
experiment to run, is the student's choice.  That holds for 51 of the 52
items of format N: running the shown code cannot tell the explanations
apart, but an experiment the student designs can.  No objective can now
be passed two of three by pasting, in any draw.  A second agent, which
did not see the first codes, coded a random 42 items: the two agreed on
39 (Cohen's $\kappa$ 0.84), all three disagreements between help and no
help.  On 12 borderline items chosen afterwards they agreed on 11; the
one they split on is the reworked item with the 12 inputs, which the
second agent coded as help.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{After the rework: the same count again}
    All 198 items coded again on the terminal's role, every item with code
    run; a second coder on 42 ($\kappa$ 0.84).
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Pasting gave the key in four of 198; the comparison had
        counted 88, at least 91 under the same coding.
      \item The four reworked the same day, after adversarial review:
        none of the 198 gives the key by pasting now.
      \item No objective can be passed two of three by pasting, in any
        draw.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

\mode<article>{\subsection{Time}\label{sec:time}}

Reasoning about code takes longer than pasting it.  We modelled the
reading time: three reader speeds and 1\,000 simulated draws of one item
per bank.  A median reader needed 47--49 minutes before the rework and
52--57 after it, with up to 26 per cent of the draws over 60 minutes.
Showing each function once, with gaps, and letting the options carry only
the lines that differ cut one bank's items from 1\,153 to 653 characters,
and from 32.6 to 12.0 lines of code, on average.  After that shortening
the median reader needed 53 minutes (95th percentile 60, 4.5 per cent of
draws over 60, 49 minutes for a reader who skims near-identical code), and
a slow reader 84.  The independent exam's own estimate of 49 minutes
matches a fast reader.  This is a model, not a measurement;
\cref{sec:early-time} compares it with the first measured times.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{Time: a model, not a measurement}
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Median reader: 47--49 minutes before the rework, 52--57
        after it.
      \item Function shown once, options only the differing lines: 1\,153
        $\to$ 653 characters per item in one bank.
      \item After shortening: median 53 minutes, 4.5\,\% of draws over
        60; a slow reader 84.
      \item Decision: 60 minutes on 26 November, two hours from next year.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

The author kept 60 minutes for the sitting on 26 November and decided on
two hours from next year: the exam must be scheduled in advance, and the
extra time for students with Funka codes R0 and R1 follows from it.

\mode<article>{\subsection{Observations from variation theory}%
\label{sec:hypotheses}}

\leavevmode\ltnote{%
  These are presented as hypotheses, with the test that could refute
  each, because they come from coding one exam against the banks, not
  from data on students.
}%
The comparison also suggested four things that variation theory makes
visible.  They are hypotheses from one comparison, not findings; each
says how the item statistics after a sitting could test it.
\begin{description}
  \item[Fusion] The two write-and-run items of the independent exam need
    several critical aspects right at once, and each distractor is the
    output of a program with exactly one aspect wrong, so the option a
    student picks names the aspect missed.  No bank item worked like
    that.  Test: whether students who pick a given distractor also fail
    the items that test that aspect.  Distractors built from
    misconceptions are an established design,%
    \autocites{Sadler1998}{Caceffo2016} not a measured gain.
  \item[The centre cue] With one bug per distractor, the key is the
    option closest to all the others: it was so in six of ten of the
    independent exam's code items, where chance gives about 2.5.  This
    convergence cue is known,\autocites{WhiteZammarelli1981}%
    {deLaffolie2017} though one study found such cues rarely led to the
    key;\autocite{Gettig2006} that one-bug distractors produce it, we
    found no source for.  Test: whether the closest option is chosen more
    often by weak students than its difficulty predicts.
  \item[Aspect-varying banks] A bank whose items test different aspects
    tests different students on different things.  Variants that change
    numbers or names stay close in difficulty,%
    \autocites{FowlerFair2022}{FowlerZilles2021} variants that change
    what is reasoned about do not.\autocite{Westacott2023}  Test: the
    spread of difficulty within each bank.
  \item[Naive tests] Three independent items, questions 4, 6 and 13
    (\cref{ep2:q4,ep2:q6,ep2:q13}), were built so that naive testing
    passes a wrong option; none in the banks.  Test: whether
    such items discriminate better than items a single run decides.
\end{description}

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{Hypotheses from variation theory}
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Fusion: each distractor is a program with one aspect wrong,
        so the choice names the aspect missed.
      \item The centre cue: one bug per distractor makes the key the
        option closest to all others (six of ten, chance 2.5).
      \item Aspect-varying banks test different students on different
        things.
      \item Items where naive testing passes a wrong option.
    \end{itemize}
    Hypotheses, to test with the item statistics after the sitting.
  \end{block}
\end{frame}


\section[Early evidence]{Early evidence: how students used the practice
  exams}
\label{sec:early}

\leavevmode\ltnote{%
  Relevant learning objectives: \cref{RapportLOFlaws,RapportLOWorkflow}.

  The section is dated and is meant to grow: more attempts will come
  before the sitting, and the sitting's own item statistics after it.
  It follows the second round because it gives a first answer to the
  two questions that round left open, how long the exam takes and
  whether the reworked items work.  The hardest items are put as
  \enquote{hard or ambiguous?}, with both readings before the author's
  verdict, so that the colleagues can weigh the readings themselves.
}%
Until students met the items, only agents and the author had checked
them.  The three practice exams are the first such meeting:
Exempelprov~1, built from the old banks; Exempelprov~2, the independent
exam; and Exempelprov~3, a draw from the rewritten banks
(\cref{app:exempelprov}).  A student may take each of them as often as
they like.  This section reports what the students did with them, from
data fetched on 25 November 2026 at 20:45, the day before the sitting.
These are practice attempts, made by the students who chose to make
them, in their own time and with whatever help they had: they show how
the items worked for those students, not how they will work in the exam.

\mode<article>{\subsection{Data and method}\label{sec:early-method}}

Two sources hold the attempts.  The submission history of each quiz
(\texttt{canvaslms submission -{}-json -H}) gives the score of every
graded attempt, and the student analysis report of New Quizzes gives,
for every attempt, each response, the points it earned and the time the
attempt took.  A response counts as right when it earned all the item's
points, and answered and right are counted separately.  The right
answers per objective are read from the score's digits, as the grading
script reads them (\cref{sec:exam}).  One item of Exempelprov~1,
question 19, gave partial credit until 23 November, so its score is
decoded with none to four quarters of the item's points taken off, and a
decoding is kept only when exactly one of them gives valid digits; two
attempts of 190 were ambiguous and are left out of the counts per
objective.  The digits agree with the report's responses on all 686
attempts that the two sources pair up by student, attempt number and
submission time.

On 23 November the author set a time limit of 60 minutes on
Exempelprov~1, which had none until then, and corrected its question~19,
the item that gave partial credit.  Of its 190 attempts, 109 began
before the change, with no time limit and the old question~19, and 81
after it.  The report keeps the old question's column beside the new
one: the 101 answers to the old question~19 are all from attempts begun
before the change, the 72 to the new one all from attempts begun after
it, and the two count as one question here.  The earlier attempts lasted
up to 36 days, so the times of Exempelprov~1 are not used; its other
figures mix the two periods.  Question~4 of Exempelprov~3, which the
author revised on 16 November, was created before the first attempt at
it.

Exempelprov~2 and~3 have 60 minutes, as the exam has.  A later attempt
does not measure the time to do the whole exam: the student has seen the
questions before, and at Exempelprov~1 a new attempt has started with
the previous attempt's answers since 23 November.  Until 25 November the
instructions of Exempelprov~2 and~3 said the same of them, although
their settings did not have that option on; that day the author changed
the sentence to say that each new attempt starts from scratch, so that
students see how long a whole exam takes them.  So times, unanswered
questions and the options chosen are counted on first attempts, where
every answer is the student's own, and a time is counted for a first
attempt that answered all 21 questions, also when the timer closed it at
60 minutes.  A count under five is given only as \enquote{fewer than
five}.

\mode<article>{\subsection{How the practice exams were used}}

Of the 130 students who tried at least one practice exam, 81 tried all
three, 17 both Exempelprov~1 and~2, and 17 both Exempelprov~2 and~3; 11
tried only Exempelprov~1, and fewer than five only Exempelprov~2 or both
Exempelprov~1 and~3.  None tried only Exempelprov~3.  The first attempt
at Exempelprov~1 is from 19 October, the first attempts at Exempelprov~2
and~3 are from 16 November.  The attempts grew towards the sitting: 40
before 16 November, 182 in the week from 16 to 22 November, 100 on 23
November, 172 on 24 November and 212 on 25 November.

