A low share right can mean that an item is hard, or that it can be read
in more than one way.  On 23 November, when the author judged them, the
three items with the lowest shares were questions 18, 9 and 20.  Each
allows a second reading, and each was run again for this section.  With
the attempts since, question~9 has the lowest share, 46 per cent, and
question~20, at 65, has risen above questions 16, 17 and 18.

Question 18 (\cref{ep3:q18}) asks which version of [[total_length_s]]
gives the total length of every playlist.  Of 138 answers, 79 chose the
key and 31 option C, which skips a song it has already counted: 22 per
cent, down from 31 on 23 November.  The two versions that start from the
first song, B and D, had 11 and 17.  On first attempts, 34 of 65 chose
the key and 15 C.  Run on an empty playlist, one song, two songs, two
equal copies of a song and the same song added twice, C differs from the
key only in the last case: the songs have no equality of their own, so
two equal copies are two songs to it.  One reading is that the item is a
hard but fair format~H: a playlist that repeats a song is a natural
case, and the feedback names it.  The other is that it is borderline:
the stem does not say that a repeated song counts twice, C is right if
it does not, and the case that separates C from the key turns on whether
two songs are the same object.  The author drew a rule for writing items
from it, rather than calling it a defect of one item: no answer may
hinge on a definition the course does not give (\cref{sec:lessons}).
The item stays as it is for now; the rule applies to new and reworked
items.

Question 20 (\cref{ep3:q20}) asks which calls of [[energy_cost]] are
suitable, that is \enquote{\foreignlanguage{swedish}{tar hand om
returvärdet (sparar det i en variabel eller använder det direkt)}} (take
care of the return value: store it in a variable or use it directly).
Of 134 answers, 31 included option A, the bare call
[[energy_cost(2.5, 1.8, 3)]], 20 of 64 on first attempts; 65 per cent of
the answers were right.  Run as a program, the call prints nothing and
its value is lost.  Typed at an interactive Python prompt, it prints
\texttt{13.5}, which a student can read as using the value directly.  The
stem says that each call \enquote{\foreignlanguage{swedish}{står för sig,
direkt efter funktionen}} (stands alone, right after the function), but
not whether in a program or at a prompt.  The exam's terminal runs the
text of its editor as a program,\autocite{Mosavat2026} so in the exam the
call would print nothing; a student who practises with another Python,
at an interactive prompt, sees the value.  The item's own feedback
mentions the interactive prompt, which the exam's terminal does not
have, as a hint.  The numbers cannot tell a misconception from a second
reading of the stem.  The author judged the item fine as it is, and it
stays unchanged.

Question 9 (\cref{ep3:q9}) asks which statements explain the printout of
an object without [[__str__]]; options A, B and C are right.  Of 144
answers, 66 were right (46 per cent).  The two most common wrong answers
each leave out one right option: AB leaves out C (25 answers), AC leaves
out B (17); every other wrong combination came seven times or fewer, and
the false option D was in 23 answers.  Those two options carry the two
wordings that may count here.  Option C says that the printout is
\enquote{\foreignlanguage{swedish}{likadan}} (the same) with private
attributes, while the stem itself says that the number in it differs
from run to run: run twice in CPython, with and without private
attributes, the printouts differ only in the address, so C is true of
the form of the printout, not of its text.  Option B says that the
address may be another the next time the program runs; whether that is
what a student sees in the exam's terminal, where Python runs in the
browser, we have not checked.  The author judged this item fine as it
is, too.

\mode<article>{\subsection{The independent exam's write-and-run
  items}\label{sec:early-write}}

Exempelprov~2 has the two items that ask the student to write and run a
program (\cref{sec:comparison}). Question 16 (\cref{ep2:q16}) asks for
the most common digit sum among the numbers 1 to 500, counted in a
dictionary; the key is the digit sum 11, which 44 of the numbers have.
According to the author, students said during the lecture on 24 November
that this question was the hardest. It had the fewest answers of the
exam, 156 in 310 attempts, and 32 of the 183 attempts that answered a
later question left it blank. The author reads this as students who saw
the question as hard and put it off; the data are consistent with that
reading but do not show it. Of the 156 answers, 132 had the right digit
sum, and 82 of those the right count. The other 50 chose 43 or 45, the
counts that two faulty counters give: one that sets a digit sum's first
count to 0, and one that counts with [[counts.get(total, 1) + 1]]. Run,
the right counter and the two faulty ones give 44, 43 and 45 for the
digit sum 11. So the digit sum was not the hard part; counting with a
dictionary was, which the item's distractors were built to separate
(\cref{sec:hypotheses}). Of the answers, 53 per cent were right, 42 per
cent on first attempts. The other write-and-run item, question~5
(\cref{ep2:q5}), did better: 140 of 201 answers right (70 per cent).

\mode<article>{\subsection{What it means for the workflow}}

The practice exams gave item statistics before the sitting, not only
after it.  The items they flagged had passed the rewriters' own checks
and the agents' reviews.  The recount after the rework coded question 20
as a near miss: the stem's own definition, a call that stores or uses
the value, rules out the bare call (\cref{sec:recount}).  An interactive
prompt that shows the value was not part of that reading, and the item's
feedback mentions it only as a hint.  The items of Exempelprov~3 are
themselves kept out of the exam's draw (\cref{app:exempelprov}), so for
the sitting the question is whether other items of the same banks share
a wording.  What the numbers cannot say is which items are flawed: for
that, a low share is a reason to read the item again, and the decision
is the author's.  Here the author kept all three items and turned what
question 18 showed into a rule for writing items.

